Operations
==========

Requirements
------------

Node 20 or newer, PostgreSQL 14 or newer. Docker is optional; docker-compose.yml
brings up the API and a Postgres instance together.


Environment
-----------

Create .env in the repository root. Every variable the code reads:

	PORT                    API port, defaults to 8000
	NODE_ENV                development | production
	CLIENT_ORIGINS          comma separated CORS allowlist. Must include the
	                        admin console origin and, for device testing, the
	                        tunnel or LAN origin the app uses.

	DB_HOST
	DB_PORT
	DB_NAME
	DB_USER
	DB_PASSWORD

	JWT_ACCESS_SECRET       signing key for short-lived access tokens
	JWT_REFRESH_SECRET      signing key for refresh tokens

	SEED_ADMIN_NAME         bootstrap admin created by runSeed
	SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL
	SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD

	SANDBOX_EMAIL_HOST      development mail catcher, e.g. a Mailtrap inbox
	SANDBOX_EMAIL_PORT
	SANDBOX_EMAIL_USER
	SANDBOX_EMAIL_PASSWORD
	EMAIL_USER              real sending account for production

Never commit .env. Keep a .env.example with the keys and no values.


Running
-------

	npm install
	npm run dev        ts-node-dev, restarts on change, transpile only
	npm run build      tsc to dist/
	npm start          node dist/index.js
	docker compose up  API plus Postgres

Startup order in src/index.ts is authenticate, defineAssociations, sync,
runSeed, listen. A failure at any step is logged and the process does not
listen, so an API that exits immediately is nearly always a database or
credentials problem.


Database lifecycle
------------------

sequelize.sync({ alter: true }) runs on every boot. There is no migration
tool. Read docs/architecture.md for the full consequences. The two failures
that will actually happen to you:

* Adding a NOT NULL column to a populated table fails with Postgres error
  23502. Either make it nullable and backfill, or truncate the table in
  development. refresh_tokens is safe to truncate; it only holds sessions and
  the effect is that everyone signs in again.
* A dropped or narrowed column may not be applied. Verify against the live
  schema rather than assuming the model is the truth.

runSeed is idempotent and can be re-run safely.


Email
-----

Verification and password reset both send six digit codes through nodemailer.
In development point SANDBOX_EMAIL_* at a mail catcher so no real mail leaves
the machine. Templates are in src/services/emailTemplates/ and share
emailLayout.ts; edit the layout once rather than each template.

Only hashes of the codes are stored, so a code cannot be recovered from the
database. If a developer needs one, read it from the mail catcher.


Connecting the clients
----------------------

	Admin console   VITE_API_PATH must end in /api
	                e.g. VITE_API_PATH=http://localhost:8000/api
	Mobile app      EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL must also end in /api and must be an
	                address the device can reach. localhost points at the
	                phone itself; use the machine's LAN address or an Expo
	                tunnel.

Both clients derive the socket origin by stripping the trailing /api. If the
base URL is wrong the symptom is a working login screen and a socket that
never connects, or the reverse.


Deployment notes
----------------

Before this runs against real student data:

* Replace sync({ alter: true }) with versioned migrations.
* Move JWT secrets into the deployment platform's secret store.
* Pin CLIENT_ORIGINS to the real origins only.
* Put the API behind HTTPS. Access tokens are bearer credentials.
* Set up a backup schedule for the Postgres instance.